<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Nutrition</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Why Is Nutrition Important?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze articles on general interest topics. Reading: Identify the main topic and related ideas in a simple structured text.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze articles about balanced diets by identifying key nutritional information (e.g., "The article mentions that fiber and protein are essential for digestion and for keeping us full.")
Can identify the main topic and related ideas in texts on healthy eating (e.g., "Explain the main topic and two benefits of eating balanced meals that include Panamanian staples, such as rice, guandú, and fish.")
Can analyze articles on food choices and their health impacts (e.g., "The article explains why whole grains are good for us. They help with digestion and give us energy.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images showing various healthy and unhealthy food options (e.g., fruits, vegetables, processed snacks, whole grains, sugary drinks). Students briefly discuss in pairs which foods they think are "good for you" and why, activating vocabulary like 'healthy,' 'processed,' 'nutrition,' and 'benefits.' The teacher then shows a large headline: "Boost Your Energy: The Power of a Balanced Diet!" and asks students to predict what kind of information they might read in an article with this title.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to quickly scan a text for keywords by pointing to the headline and showing how certain words like 'energy' and 'balanced diet' hint at the main topic. The teacher also models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from the previous lesson, focusing on stress in words like "nutrition" and "carbohydrates," and minimal pairs like /ɪ/ in "bit" (as in 'vitamins') and /iː/ in "beat" (as in 'protein').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a headline says 'Boost Your Energy,' will the article be about sleeping or eating well?" (Expected answer: Eating well.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'balanced diet' mean? Does it mean eating only one type of food?" (Expected answer: No, it means eating a mix of foods.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nutrition".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, informative article titled "Fueling Your Body: Why Good Nutrition is Key for Teenagers." The article (approx. 150-200 words) is structured with clear paragraphs, each addressing a benefit of good nutrition, and includes target grammar (modal 'should,' first conditional, adverbial clauses for justification) and vocabulary (nutrition, health, benefits, balanced diet, fruits, vegetables, grains, vitamins, minerals, fiber, protein, carbohydrates, digestion). The register is informational and accessible for 9th graders.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Analyze articles on general interest topics. Reading: Identify the main topic and related ideas in a simple structured text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> Students read the article silently and choose the best main idea from two or three options provided by the teacher (e.g., a) This article explains how to cook healthy meals. b) This article explains why good nutrition helps teenagers stay healthy and active. c) This article talks about different types of exercise.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read specific statements and decide if they are True or False based on the text (e.g., "The article says processed foods are superfoods." "According to the text, fiber helps with digestion.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match key nutrients (e.g., 'protein,' 'carbohydrates,' 'vitamins') to their main benefits as described in the article.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension focused on identifying specific information and understanding the use of modal "should" for advice, first conditional for facts, and adverbial clauses for justification. Reinforcing vocabulary related to nutrition and health.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Gap-fill:</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences extracted from the article, but with key vocabulary words (e.g., 'nutrients,' 'digestion,' 'beneficial,' 'avoid') or grammatical structures (e.g., 'should,' 'if you eat well,' 'because it') missing. They fill in the blanks using a word bank or by recalling from the article. (e.g., "You ______ eat more fruits and vegetables because they are full of ______.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Sentence Transformation:</b> Students are given sentences from the article and asked to rephrase them using a specific target structure. For example, "Eating fruits and vegetables helps you stay healthy" becomes "If you eat fruits and vegetables, you will stay healthy." Or, "It's a good idea to consume whole grains" becomes "You should consume whole grains." This activity allows them to manipulate language while referring to the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Detail Hunt:</b> In pairs, students receive a list of specific questions related to the article's details (e.g., "What does the article say about the benefits of fiber?" "Why is a balanced diet important for concentration?"). They reread the article together to find and highlight the answers, identifying the exact sentences or phrases that justify their answers. This encourages collaborative reading and justification using adverbial clauses.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nutrition".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Nutrition Tips for Teens" Mini-Poster.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a short, additional article or infographic (A2.4 level) about specific food groups or common Panamanian staple foods (e.g., rice, guandú, fish) and their nutritional contributions. Their task is to read and analyze this new information, then create a mini-poster with 3-4 "Top Nutrition Tips for Teens." Each tip must be written as advice (using "You should...") and include a justification (using "because it...") or a conditional statement (using "If you..., you will..."), directly reflecting information from the texts they've read. They must use at least 5 new vocabulary words from the lesson and practice stress in multisyllabic words like "carbohydrate" when discussing their poster.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Tip:</b> "You should eat more whole grains like rice and oats because they provide fiber, which helps with digestion. If you consume a balanced breakfast, you will stay focused longer during class."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and a quick review of the "Nutrition Tips for Teens" mini-posters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their mini-posters to the class (1-2 minutes per group) or display them. The teacher assesses their ability to extract and synthesize information from the provided texts to create meaningful nutrition advice, effectively using target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of nutritional information derived from the articles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of modal "should" for advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of first conditional for facts/outcomes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of adverbial clauses for justification ("because it...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., nutrition, benefits, fiber, protein, balanced diet).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and legibility of the tips on the poster.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration within the group during the task.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the main topic of the first article we read today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one food or nutrient mentioned in today's texts that is beneficial for your health, and explain why (e.g., 'You should eat ______ because it ______')."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new information about nutrition did you learn from reading today's articles?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How easy or difficult was it to find the main idea and specific details in the texts?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students successfully analyze the articles and identify key nutritional information and main topics? Were the texts appropriate for A2.4?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which activities effectively promoted reading comprehension and the use of target grammar/vocabulary? What adjustments might be needed for supporting students with identifying related ideas?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson, we will use the nutrition tips and ideas we read about today to practice offering dietary advice and discussing healthy eating habits in various interactive conversations."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>